# Mobilis 迁跃

[![tests](https://github.com/xiaoshentu/Mobilis/actions/workflows/tests.yml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/xiaoshentu/Mobilis/actions/workflows/tests.yml)
[![DOI](https://zenodo.org/badge/DOI/10.5281/zenodo.23593488.svg)](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23593488)
[![License: GPL v3](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-GPLv3-blue.svg)](LICENSE)

**A charge-transport and photophysics toolkit for organic semiconductors** — from a crystal structure or an MD
morphology to carrier mobilities, with a Tkinter GUI and a full command-line interface.

Developer: **Yarui Shi**, Anyang Normal University (安阳师范学院). 中文说明见 [README_zh.md](README_zh.md).

*Mobilis* is Latin for "movable", the root of *mobility*; 迁跃 combines 迁移 (migration) and 跃迁 (transition).
The Python package is `mobility_tool`.

## What it does

| Area | Features |
|---|---|
| Site energies | Four-point reorganization energy, IP/EA/HEP/EEP, HOMO/LUMO; λ decomposition (Huang–Rhys factors, Duschinsky matrix, internal coordinates, atoms) built-in or via Gaussian FCHT |
| Transfer integrals | Gaussian dimer projection, ADF fragment orbitals, built-in extended Hückel (EHT), ZINDO/MOO; method comparison and DFT calibration of semi-empirical couplings |
| Polarization | Atomic charges + Thole induced dipoles: P±, solid-state IE/EA, site-energy differences and σ_E |
| Transport models | Marcus/Jortner rates, mobility tensors and angular mobility, crystal-graph KMC (with exact-solution self-check), transient localization theory (TLT) with effective masses |
| Dynamic disorder (CEDS) | Coupling–energy dual surface: DFT coupling surfaces over intermolecular coordinates; thermal sampling by dimer MC, U_eq-calibrated MC, or full-crystal rigid-body MD; ⟨V⟩, σ_V, τ_c → TLT, μ(T) |
| Active learning | Gaussian-process coupling surfaces with symmetry augmentation; batch IMSE selection of the next DFT configurations; posterior intervals on ⟨V⟩ and σ_V as a stopping criterion; frame-resolved TLT with the couplings of every bond in every MD frame |
| Morphologies | GROMACS morphology → molecule mapping → neighbour list → couplings → site energies → KMC; CIF → .gro supercells |
| Photophysics | S0/S1/T1 inputs, absorption/fluorescence/phosphorescence, Stokes shift, vibronically resolved phosphorescence, energy-gap law |
| Workflow | Batch scan projects (levels, grids, validation sets, symmetry checks, reports, archives); coupling-surface database; local job runner |

External quantum-chemistry codes (Gaussian, ADF/AMS, xtb, GROMACS) are optional — Mobilis writes their inputs
and reads their outputs; EHT, KMC, TLT, CEDS sampling and the GP surfaces run inside Mobilis.

## Install

```bash
pip install mobilis-1.31.0-py3-none-any.whl     # or, from the source folder: pip install .   (pip install ".[fast]" adds numba)
conda env create -f environment.yml             # conda alternative; packaging/conda/meta.yaml is a conda-build recipe
mobilis --version                               # CLI (same as python -m mobility_tool)
mobilis-gui                                     # GUI (same as python run_gui.py)
```

Step-by-step tutorials (Chinese) with demo files are in [docs/tutorials](docs/tutorials/README.md).

Python ≥ 3.9. The GUI uses tkinter (bundled with the official Windows/macOS Python; on Linux
`sudo apt install python3-tk`). Reading ADF binary results needs PLAMS (`pip install plams`, or start Mobilis
with `$AMSBIN/amspython run_gui.py`). A Windows installer can be built with the scripts in `packaging/`.

## Quick examples

```bash
# Marcus hopping mobility from a CIF using the built-in EHT couplings
python -m mobility_tool kmc pentacene.cif --lam 0.092 --carrier hole
python -m mobility_tool tlt pentacene.cif --carrier hole

# CEDS with active learning: each round writes 10–20 ADF inputs; run them, then rerun the same command
python -m mobility_tool ceds al --cif pentacene.cif --project P --name pen --pairs 1,2,3,7
# ... when every pair reports converged:
python -m mobility_tool ceds md --cif pentacene.cif --project P --al-surfaces pen
```

Run `python -m mobility_tool <command> -h` for all options. Help and messages are in English; for Chinese use
`python -m mobility_tool --lang zh <command> ...` or set `MOBILIS_LANG=zh` (the GUI language is switched in the
interface). The full command reference is in [docs/USER_MANUAL.md](docs/USER_MANUAL.md).

## Worked example: mobility tensor of a molecular crystal

```bash
# 1  neighbour dimers within 4 Å (shortest atom-atom distance), grouped by symmetry, with Gaussian inputs
python -m mobility_tool crystal anthracene.cif --cutoff 4.0 -o dimers --program gaussian --method B3LYP --basis "6-31G(d)"
python -m mobility_tool reorg-gen dimers/G1_A.gjf -o reorg --name anthracene --carrier both
# 2  run the Gaussian jobs (dimers/run_ti.bat, reorg/run_reorg.bat), or locally:  python -m mobility_tool run dimers
# 3  reorganization energy and the mobility tensor
python -m mobility_tool reorg-analyze reorg --json anthracene_lambda.json
python -m mobility_tool mobility --dimers dimers --lam 0.137 --carrier hole --dim 3 --cif anthracene.cif
```

The angle-resolved mobility uses the projection of the random-walk diffusion tensor, μ(φ) = e/(2k_BT) Σ W r² cos²γ cos²(θ − φ).
The formula of Wen et al. (J. Phys. Chem. B 2009), which multiplies each pathway by its hopping probability, is kept as
`mobility2d --model wen` for comparison with published values; on a one-dimensional chain it gives half of the correct value
(`python -m mobility_tool mobility --lam 0.2 --path 5.0,30,2`). For cells whose molecules do not sit on inversion centres the
exact random walk on the crystal graph is solved from the homogenized rate equations; `kmc` converges to the same limit.

## Test run

`examples/test_run/run_test.py` runs a complete calculation without Gaussian or ADF (KMC against exact solutions,
dimer search and input generation, EHT/KMC/TLT, and the four-crystal anisotropy benchmark from stored B3LYP results)
in about 40 s and compares every number with `examples/test_run/expected_output/`.

## Repository layout

| Path | Contents |
|---|---|
| `mobility_tool/` | the Python package: `cli.py` (command line), `gui.py` (graphical interface), `crystal.py` (CIF, molecules, dimers, symmetry), `reorganization.py`, `transfer_integral.py`, `adf.py`, `eht.py`, `zindo.py` (quantum-chemistry inputs, outputs and built-in couplings), `mobility.py`, `transport.py`, `kmc.py`, `validation.py` (transport models; transient localization in `transport.py`), `polar.py` (site energies), `ceds.py`, `rigidmd.py`, `alsurface.py`, `phonons.py`, `frametlt.py` (dynamic disorder), `photocond.py` (photoconductivity), `database.py` (coupling-surface database), `morphology.py` (MD morphologies), plotting and analysis modules |
| `tests/` | test suite (`run_all.py`) with simulated Gaussian/ADF executables and test data |
| `examples/test_run/` | test run with inputs, driver script and expected output |
| `examples/` | small input examples (configuration file, dimer structures, pathway table) |
| `docs/USER_MANUAL.md` | user manual and command reference |
| `packaging/` | scripts for a Windows installer |
| `run_gui.py`, `Mobilis.bat` | start the graphical interface |

## Changes in 1.31.0

- **Excited-state rates from thermal vibration correlation functions** (`tvcf.py`): harmonic states with frequency
  changes and full Duschinsky rotation built from two freq fchk files; radiative rate and emission spectrum (FC and
  Herzberg–Teller), internal conversion (promoting modes, non-adiabatic coupling vector), intersystem and reverse
  intersystem crossing (spin–orbit coupling, FC and spin-vibronic). Commands `tvcf` (one process) and `tadf`
  (S0/S1/T1: k_r, k_IC, k_ISC, k_RISC, T1 decay, then the S1/T1 kinetics with prompt/delayed lifetimes and quantum
  yields); GUI section on the excited-state page. Checked against exact sums over vibrational states (1D and 2D,
  frequency change, Duschinsky rotation, HT, promoting modes; deviations 1e-13 to 3e-5), against the displaced-
  oscillator FGR rate and against detailed balance (`tests/test_v52.py`). 174 modes take about 2 minutes.
- **Deformation-potential band mobility** (`dp.py`): `dp-gen` writes strained cells (CIF or POSCAR) and the strain
  table, `dp` fits the elastic constant C and the deformation potential E₁, and gives the Bardeen–Shockley mobility
  (3D and 2D) and Boltzmann transport on the tight-binding band of the crystal with the DP relaxation time (no
  parabolic-band assumption). The Boltzmann code reproduces Bardeen–Shockley for parabolic bands (`tests/test_v53.py`).
- **Project files** (`*.mobilis.json`, `project.py`): `project new` (templates crystal / emitter / deformation),
  `project check` (every step parsed by the real argument parser), `project run` (logs and status per step,
  `--only`, `--start`, `--set`), `project show`. GUI: File → Save project / Open project keeps every setting of every
  page.
- **Tutorials**: six step-by-step tutorials in `docs/tutorials` with demo files in `examples/` (`tests/test_v54.py`
  parses every tutorial command and runs the ones that use the demo files).
- **pip / conda**: wheel with the data files (atomic densities were missing from the package before), entry points
  `mobilis` and `mobilis-gui`, extras `[fast]`, `[md]`, `[all]`; `environment.yml` and a conda-build recipe.

## Changes in 1.30.0

- **Quantum nuclear tunneling (Fermi golden rule over all modes).** New module `tunneling.py`: the hopping rate
  k = |V|²/ħ² ∫ dt exp[iΔG t/ħ − G(t)] with the Huang–Rhys factors of every intramolecular mode of both charge
  states (the two `*_modes.csv` files of `reorg-decomp`) and an optional classical part (outer sphere, or modes below
  `--classical-below`). It reduces to Marcus when all modes are classical and to Marcus–Levich–Jortner for one
  high-frequency mode (both within 0.2 %, `tests/test_v51.py`).
- New command `tunneling --modes X_neutral_modes.csv X_ion_modes.csv --cif X.cif --temps 100,200,300`: k_FGR/k_Marcus,
  k_MLJ/k_Marcus and the zero-field mobility (analytic tensor, optionally KMC with `--kmc`) at each temperature, written
  to CSV. `kmc --rate fgr --modes …` uses the same rates in the crystal-graph KMC (field, energetic disorder).
- The comparison page of the GUI and `compare_methods` can add the rows "FGR tensor" and "KMC (FGR)" (check box
  "quantum nuclear tunneling"), with the modes of the decomposition page.

## Changes in 1.29.1

- One language per run: with the Chinese interface, messages and help texts written in English are now shown in
  Chinese, and with the English interface the remaining Chinese messages are translated (≈ 400 new entries,
  `_cli_i18n_more.py`, including argparse's own texts and the reports of `diagnose` and frame-resolved TLT).
  `tests/test_v50.py` checks every message and help text of the code in both languages and runs `diagnose` and two
  help pages end to end, so a new message without a translation fails the tests.
- The EHT worker processes are closed at exit (no "Exception ignored ... weakref_cb" on Windows).

## Changes in 1.29.0

- **ORCA and xtb levels** for scans and active learning (`scan level --engine orca|xtb`): ORCA with any DFT method
  and basis (`--method "B3LYP D3BJ" --qc-basis def2-SVP`, free for academic use), xtb with GFN1/GFN2 (seconds per
  dimer). Monomer A is computed once (`run_mono.sh` / `.bat` → `mono.molden.input`); every dimer needs B and AB.
  The coupling is the dimer projection on the Molden files (`orca_2mkl -molden`, `xtb --molden`); the phase of B is
  aligned to the reference monomer through its p coefficients, so the signs of V are consistent over a scan as with
  the frozen ADF fragments. Batch files for bash and Windows; precheck, collect, surfaces and `ceds al` work as for
  ADF. Program paths from `ORCA_EXE` / `XTB_EXE` when not on PATH.
- **Faster EHT baseline with numba** (optional, `pip install numba`): the intermolecular overlap block runs as a
  compiled kernel with primitive screening (results equal to machine precision); without numba the numpy code is used.
  `python tools_bench_eht.py crystal.cif` measures both on your computer. `EHTBaseline` prepares monomer A and the
  basis once per surface.

## Changes in 1.28.3

- Parallel EHT baseline: the worker processes now run with one BLAS/LAPACK thread each. Before, every worker started
  as many BLAS threads as there are cores (MKL / OpenBLAS default), so with all cores in use the machine ran J × J
  threads and the parallel EHT could become slower than one process (rubrene MD couplings: hours instead of minutes).
  Batches of 50 000 or more configurations report progress. Numbers are unchanged.

## Changes in 1.28.2

- `ceds md`: the coupling statistics after the MD report every pair and, for pairs with 20 000 or more
  configurations, progress and remaining time (with a Δ-GP surface every configuration needs the EHT baseline:
  rubrene 1×12×8 has 1.4×10^5 configurations per pair). Numbers are unchanged.
- `ceds md`: trajectories are saved by default to `md_traj_<cif>_<supercell>` in the working folder together with
  their settings (temperature, supercell, MD settings, force field, charges, lattice) and reused when the same run is
  started again, so an interrupted or repeated analysis does not rerun the MD. `--rerun-md` forces a new MD,
  `--no-save-traj` keeps nothing, `--save-traj DIR` chooses the folder.

## Changes in 1.28.1

- `ceds md --frame-tlt`: the coupling statistics (Gaussian and marginal draws, shape, correlations) use about 300 MD
  frames (`--frame-stride K` sets every K-th frame; 30 ps at 20 fs gives K = 5); the frame-resolved Hamiltonians are
  the same N frames as before. Progress per bond with the remaining time.
- EHT baseline of Δ-GP surfaces: large batches (MD couplings, frame-resolved TLT) run in worker processes
  (`--jobs N`, default all CPU cores). Rubrene with a 1×12×8 supercell: the frame-TLT step falls from about 13 h to
  well under 1 h on 8 cores.

## Changes in 1.28.0

- `exciton`: singlet-exciton transport. Transition charges from a built-in INDO/S–CIS calculation or read from a
  Gaussian TD-DFT log (`--charges log`, input written with `--write-gjf`), TrESP couplings of all neighbour pairs
  of the crystal (optical screening f = 1/n²), the k = 0 Davydov splitting, Marcus exciton hopping rates
  (`--lambda`), the diffusion tensor, the diffusion length L = (2Dτ)^1/2 (`--tau`) and a kinetic Monte Carlo check
  (`--kmc`). `--md-ps` adds rigid-body MD of the crystal and the thermal statistics ⟨V⟩, σ_V of the exciton couplings.
- `ceds al --al-calib-report`: report-only check of the interval calibration. For every pair: c_LOO of each carrier,
  the fraction of standardized leave-one-out residuals within ±1.96, and the 95% half-width of σ_V for c = 1.5, 2.0,
  2.5 and c_LOO. Writes `<name>_calib_report.json`; no new round is written.
- `ceds md`: lattice-stability check at every temperature — U_eq against the crystallographic value of the CIF
  (warning above 2×), growth of U_eq between the two halves of the production run (above 1.3×), displacement of the
  molecular centres between the first and last quarter (above 0.5 Å) and, over a temperature series, U_eq/T against
  the lowest temperature (above 1.5×; a harmonic lattice has U_eq ∝ T). Flagged temperatures are marked in the
  summary and in the CSV (`U_eq_growth`, `drift_max_A`, `lattice_stable`).

## Changes in 1.27.0

- `photocond`: photogenerated carriers and photoconductivity under illumination. Input: excitation wavelength,
  absorption coefficient, film thickness and reflectance, intensity (one or several values, W cm⁻²) or pulse energy
  density, electric field, temperature, the electron and hole mobilities (given, or read from a `ceds md` result at the
  temperature), the permittivity, the exciton binding energy (`--eb`, or `--et` and `--eopt`) and the singlet-exciton
  lifetime. Output per intensity and field: exciton generation rate, free-carrier yield η (Onsager–Braun dissociation
  in competition with exciton decay, e.g. singlet fission), free-carrier generation G = η g, steady density n from
  Langevin recombination (reduction factor `--zeta`) and optional trap recombination (`--tau-trap`), carrier
  lifetime, Δσ = e n (μe + μh) and the photocurrent density. With several intensities the local exponent k of
  Δσ ∝ I^k is printed (0.5: bimolecular Langevin recombination, 1: monomolecular). `--fluence` adds the decay of Δσ
  after a short pulse. The same calculation is a new GUI page, Photoconductivity (Mobility menu): mobilities typed in
  or read from a `ceds md` result, ε from the Polarization page, a results table with the exponent k, plots of Δσ
  against intensity, η against field and Δσ(t) after a pulse, and CSV/PNG export.
- `ceds al --al-calib loo`: the calibration factor of the posterior intervals is taken for each pair from the
  standardized leave-one-out residuals of its DFT points (all symmetry images of a point are left out together;
  closed form with fixed hyperparameters and re-estimated baseline coefficients). The default remains the fixed
  factor c = 2.0; the report now lists c_LOO for every pair in both modes.
- `ceds md`: progress of the MD of each temperature every 5% of the steps, with elapsed and estimated remaining time.

## Changes in 1.26.0

- `ceds md --frame-tlt [N]`: transient localization with the Hamiltonians of N MD frames (default 24) on the MD
  supercell. On the same lattice the mobility is also computed with independent Gaussian draws N(⟨V⟩, σ_V) per bond
  (standard TLT) and with independent draws from the MD distribution of each bond type; the differences separate the
  effect of the non-Gaussian shape of the coupling distribution from that of correlations between bonds. The report
  gives skewness and excess kurtosis of every bond type and the correlation of the couplings of bonds that share a
  molecule; `<out>_frames.csv` holds the tables. The supercell needs at least 8 cells along both transport directions
  (12–16 recommended, e.g. `--supercell 12,12,2`). Not available for crystals with enantiomeric molecules.
- `db add / list / export`: coupling-surface database, one folder per crystal with the finished DFT dimers
  (`dimers.csv`, the same columns as `all_results.csv`, energy decomposition included), their coordinates
  (`structures.xyz`) and `record.json` (formula, point group, cell, space group, CCDC number, levels of theory,
  active-learning rounds and DFT points per pair, reorganization energies, MD pair statistics, μ(T), snapshot
  validation). `--no-cif` stores the cell and the deposition number without the structure file. `db export` merges the
  dimers of all crystals into one table for model training.
- Rigid-body MD: the neighbour list is built from molecular centroid distances first (same pair list, much faster for
  large supercells).

## Changes in 1.25.0

- `diagnose --uq`: the transport regime as a probability. The largest scaled EHT coupling is drawn from its error
  against B3LYP dimer projection in the four-crystal benchmark, λ and the phonon σ_J/|J| from log-normal distributions;
  the report gives P(hopping), P(transient localization), P(band-like), the 90 % interval of 2|J|/λ and the input that
  dominates the uncertainty (`--lam-rel`, `--uq-samples`, `--seed`).
- Scan projects: a scan folder deleted by hand is skipped with a warning instead of stopping `ceds`.

## Changes in 1.24.0

- Command-line help and messages in English (Chinese with `--lang zh` or `MOBILIS_LANG=zh`).
- `kmc-validate --cif`: adds the crystal-graph case (KMC against the homogenized exact solution) for any crystal.
- `examples/test_run`: complete test run with expected output; `docs/USER_MANUAL.md`: user manual and command reference.
- `diagnose`: transport regime from a CIF and the two reorganization energies in a few seconds (built-in EHT couplings and harmonic rigid-body phonons): 2|J|/λ, σ_J/|J|, interlayer/in-plane coupling ratio and π character of the frontier orbitals, with the recommended model and command.
- π frontier orbitals: EHT reports the π character of HOMO/LUMO and warns when a lone-pair σ orbital lies at the frontier (aza-aromatics); `--pi` (`eht-ti`, `kmc`, `tlt`; `pi=True` in Python) uses the highest occupied / lowest unoccupied π orbital instead.
- CIF reader accepts blank lines inside loop headers.
- Molecular frames: when the monomer (for example a gas-phase optimized structure) fits a crystal molecule poorly on its principal axes (pseudo-symmetric molecules such as mer-Alq3, whose principal moments are nearly degenerate), the frame is refined by a least-squares fit of the heavy atoms. Before, such molecules could be placed rotated by tens of degrees in the crystal (positive MD lattice energy).
- ZINDO couplings of molecules that coincide with the reference only after an improper operation (inversion/mirror images of non-planar molecules) fall back to a full dimer INDO/S projection instead of stopping.

The full change history is in [README_zh.md](README_zh.md) (Chinese).

## Tests

```bash
python tests/run_all.py          # all tests (no Gaussian/ADF needed: fake programs are used)
python tests/run_all.py --quick  # skips the slow GUI smoke test and test_v18 (~6 min less)
```

